1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,700
This is Sensorium.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:14,700
Your model reads text and images, but real work runs on 3D scans, video, molecules and GPS tracks. Sensorium gives the model you already use a new sense, with proof first.

3
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:22,700
Here's Claude with a 3D file. It never reaches the model. File not received.

4
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,700
So we ask the agent. One file, one sentence.

5
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:36,700
It detects a 3D model, designs an adapter that renders it, and writes the code in a sandbox with no network.

6
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,700
This is the code it wrote. It ran first time.

7
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,700
Then it goes looking for test data,

8
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:59,700
on the Hugging Face Hub and across the web with Linkup. It finds labelled ModelNet meshes, and a second set of objects it was never built around.

9
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:07,700
Now Claude answers fifty held-out questions through the adapter, and again under controls.

10
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:19,700
One hundred percent. With another file's input: eight. Reading the raw file: thirty-six. Categories it never saw: seventy-two. Every bar cleared, so it installs.

11
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:29,700
Same model, same question. Now it sees a chair, and says which sense carried the file. The model's weights never changed.

12
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:37,700
Under the hood: a frozen model, sandboxed adapters, and fixed rules that decide. Linkup finds the data. Databricks records the runs.

13
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:49,700
We designed it in Figma. Every variable maps to the app's CSS, and every component has an installed and a refused state, with equal weight.

14
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,700
It also says no. Claude already reads coordinates.

15
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:59,700
Thirty-three formats, anything else from scratch. 3D: three for three. Sensorium.
